    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          People take treadmills apart for three main reasons: to get one through a doorway or up a
          staircase, to move house, or to break one down for disposal. All three need less
          dismantling than most people expect, and all three go wrong in the same ways — a console
          cable pulled out of its socket, a bag of unlabelled bolts, an upright dropped on a foot.
          This page covers how far to go for each purpose, the tools and safety precautions, the
          order that keeps the wiring intact, and how to put it back together so that it works.
          The steps are general; your manual's assembly section, read backwards, is the specific
          version for your machine.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. How Do You Disassemble a Treadmill? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Unplug the treadmill, photograph everything, disconnect the console wiring
            harness at its connector, then unbolt and lift off the console and the uprights, and
            fold and lock the frame or separate the deck if you need to go further.</strong> In
            most cases that is as far as you need to go: the motor, the control board and the deck
            can stay where they are.
          </p>
          <p>
            The order matters because of one cable. On most treadmills, the console is connected to
            the motor control board in the base by a wiring harness that runs up through one of the
            uprights. If you unbolt the uprights before disconnecting that harness, the weight of
            the console and upright ends up hanging on the cable, and a torn connector or a pinched
            wire is the commonest reason a disassembled treadmill will not work afterwards.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three habits make the difference:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Photograph each stage</strong> before you undo it, especially every
            connector.</li>
            <li><strong>Bag and label the bolts</strong> for each part, and tape the bag to that part.</li>
            <li><strong>Work with two people</strong> for anything that has to be lifted.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A typical disassembly for moving — console off, uprights off, frame folded or flat —
            commonly takes somewhere around an hour for someone working carefully for the first
            time. Taking the deck and motor out as well takes longer and is rarely necessary. If you
            are moving the machine rather than taking it apart for its own sake, our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to move a treadmill</a>
            covers the route, the lift and the van.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-far" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. How Far Do You Actually Need to Take It Apart?</h2>
<p>
            Less than you think, in most cases. Decide what the disassembly is for before starting,
            because every bolt you remove is one you have to replace correctly.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Purpose</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Typically enough</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Usually leave alone</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moving within the house, level floors</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fold and lock, or nothing at all</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Everything</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Narrow doorway or tight turn</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console and uprights off</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor hood, deck, belt</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stairs</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console and uprights off; deck folded and strapped or base carried flat</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Control board, motor</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moving house</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console and uprights off if the van or route needs it</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor hood, deck</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Disposal or scrap</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">As far as the collector requires; often just enough to carry it out</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Control board until unplugged and discharged</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            For a folding treadmill on a level route, folding it and locking the deck may be all you
            need. For a non-folding treadmill, removing the console and uprights turns a tall,
            awkward object into a long, flat one, which is what usually gets it through doorways
            and around stair landings. Separating the deck from the base, or taking the motor out,
            is for heavy machines on difficult staircases, for repairs, or for scrap.
          </p>
          <p>
            For disposal, check what the collector actually wants. Many municipal bulk pickups and
            haul-away services take a treadmill whole, and some scrap or e-waste recyclers prefer
            the electronics separated. Our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-dispose-of-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to dispose of a treadmill</a>
            covers the options; there is no point dismantling a machine further than its next owner
            or collector needs.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Taking apart a NordicTrack, ProForm or other brand</h3>
          <p>
            The general sequence on this page applies to most brands, including NordicTrack and
            ProForm, but the fastener positions, cover designs and folding mechanisms vary from
            model to model. Find the model number, usually on a label on the frame, and download
            the manual for that model: its assembly section, read in reverse, is the most reliable
            disassembly guide you will find. Our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-reset-nordictrack-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">resetting a NordicTrack treadmill</a>
            includes notes on taking one apart for a move and the checks afterwards.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Tools Do You Need to Take a Treadmill Apart?</h2>
<p>
            Ordinary hand tools, plus a few things for staying organised. Most treadmills are put
            together with hex-head or Allen bolts and a few Phillips screws, and the tools that came
            in the assembly kit will often undo them, though a proper set is easier on the hands.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Hex keys (Allen keys)</strong> in a range of sizes; a set with a T-handle or
            ball end helps with bolts in awkward spots.</li>
            <li><strong>A socket set and ratchet,</strong> metric and imperial if you are unsure
            which your machine uses, with an extension bar for recessed bolts.</li>
            <li><strong>Phillips and flat screwdrivers</strong> for covers, the motor hood and
            console panels.</li>
            <li><strong>An adjustable wrench</strong> for holding nuts while you turn the bolt.</li>
            <li><strong>Resealable bags, masking tape and a marker</strong> for labelling bolts and
            cables. This is not optional.</li>
            <li><strong>A phone camera</strong> for photographing every stage and connector.</li>
            <li><strong>Work gloves</strong> for sharp frame edges and pinch points.</li>
            <li><strong>Moving blankets or cardboard</strong> to protect the floor and the parts.</li>
            <li><strong>Cable ties or tape</strong> to secure loose cables to the frame.</li>
            <li><strong>A torch</strong> for looking into the base and under the console.</li>
            <li><strong>Ratchet straps</strong> if the deck is going to stay folded for transport.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A furniture dolly and a second person are the other two things that matter. Neither is a
            tool in the usual sense, but the base of a motorised treadmill is the heaviest single
            part and commonly needs two people to carry safely.
          </p>
          <p>
            You do not need electrical test equipment, and nothing in a disassembly for moving
            should require touching anything that would need it. If you find yourself reaching for
            a multimeter, you have moved from disassembly into repair, and the advice changes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What Safety Precautions Matter Most?</h2>
<p>
            Disconnect the power, respect the weight, and stay away from the controller. Those three
            cover most of the ways people get hurt or damage the machine.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Power</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Lower the incline to zero first,</strong> while the machine is still powered,
            if it has a powered incline. A deck left at an incline is awkward to fold and carry, and
            some incline mechanisms are harder to handle when raised.</li>
            <li><strong>Switch off, remove the safety key and unplug at the wall.</strong> Then
            unplug the power cord from the treadmill itself if it is detachable, and coil it away.</li>
            <li><strong>Wait before opening the motor hood.</strong> Capacitors on the motor control
            board can store a charge after the power is removed. A wait of around half an hour is a
            common precaution; even then, do not touch the board, its terminals or the wiring
            attached to it.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Weight and pinch points</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Two people for every lift.</strong> Most of a treadmill's weight is in the
            base at the motor end, and an upright with the console attached is heavy and
            top-heavy.</li>
            <li><strong>Support parts before the last bolt comes out.</strong> An upright or console
            that drops when its final bolt is removed can land on a foot or tear the cable.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep fingers out of the folding mechanism and rollers.</strong> The hinge,
            the latch and the gap between deck and frame are classic pinch points.</li>
            <li><strong>Lift with your legs</strong> and do not twist while carrying.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Stored energy</h3>
          <p>
            Folding treadmills with a soft-drop feature use a gas strut or hydraulic cylinder to
            control the deck. These hold energy. Do not disconnect a strut while it is supporting
            the deck, and do not try to dismantle one. If the deck must be separated from the frame,
            follow the manual's sequence for releasing it, and if the manual does not cover it,
            leave the strut connected and move the base as one piece.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, keep children and pets out of the room. A half-dismantled treadmill is full
            of loose bolts, open tubes and parts that can tip.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="labelling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How Do You Keep Track of the Bolts and Cables?</h2>
<p>
            Photograph first, bag second, label everything. The bolts on a treadmill look alike but
            are often different lengths and threads, and putting a longer bolt into a hole meant for
            a shorter one can damage a thread, crush a cable or bottom out against something inside
            the tube.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Photograph each stage before undoing it,</strong> from more than one angle.
            Include close-ups of every cable connector, showing which way round it fits and any
            colour markings.</li>
            <li><strong>One bag per part.</strong> Console bolts in one, left upright in another,
            right upright in a third, base and frame in a fourth. Write what they are on the bag.</li>
            <li><strong>Tape the bag to the part it came from.</strong> Bags kept in a box get
            mixed up in the move; bags taped to the right upright arrive with the right
            upright.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep washers in order.</strong> Some bolts have a flat washer and a spring
            washer, or a spacer, in a specific order. Thread them back onto the bolt in that order
            before bagging it.</li>
            <li><strong>Mark left and right.</strong> Uprights and side covers are often handed and
            only fit one way. A strip of tape marked L or R saves guesswork.</li>
            <li><strong>Note anything unusual.</strong> A missing bolt, a stripped thread, a cable
            that was already pinched — write it down so you do not blame yourself later or forget
            to order a replacement.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you still have the original assembly hardware card or the manual's parts list, it
            identifies each bolt by length and position. That turns reassembly from guesswork into
            matching, and is worth keeping with the bags.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console-cable" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Step 1: How Do You Disconnect the Console Wiring Harness?</h2>
<p>
            Find the connector where the cable from the base meets the console, and separate it at
            the connector, never by pulling on the wire. This comes first, before any structural
            bolt is removed.
          </p>
          <p>
            On many treadmills the connection is behind a rear cover or access panel on the console,
            or at the top of an upright just below it. Remove the cover screws and look for one or
            more multi-pin plugs, sometimes with a separate earth wire. On some machines there is
            also a connector at the bottom of the upright where the cable leaves the base, and the
            manual may tell you to disconnect there instead. Check the assembly section to see where
            the cable was connected when the machine was built.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Photograph the connection</strong> as it is, including which way round the
            plug fits.</li>
            <li><strong>Release the latch.</strong> Most connectors have a small tab or clip that
            has to be pressed before they will separate. Press it and pull the plug body straight
            apart.</li>
            <li><strong>Label both halves</strong> if there is more than one connector.</li>
            <li><strong>Secure the base end of the cable.</strong> Tape it to the upright or tie a
            length of string to it, so it does not slide down inside the upright tube. Fishing a
            cable back up through a steel tube is one of the most frustrating parts of
            reassembly.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Some consoles have additional cables for heart-rate grips, speakers, a fan or a tablet
            holder. Disconnect and label each one in the same way. If a connector will not release,
            do not force it; look again for the latch, because a broken latch means a connector
            that may work loose later.
          </p>
          <p>
            This cable is also where most problems after reassembly come from. Our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to assemble a treadmill</a>
            explains why it gets pinched and how to route it when it goes back together.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console-uprights" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Step 2: How Do You Remove the Console and Uprights?</h2>
<p>
            With the cable disconnected, support the console and remove the bolts holding it to the
            uprights, then remove the uprights from the base. On some machines the console and
            uprights come off as one assembly; on others the console comes off first.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Support the console.</strong> One person holds it while the other removes
            the bolts. Consoles with large screens are heavier than they look.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove the console bolts</strong> and lift the console away. Lay it face-up
            on a blanket, screen protected, somewhere it will not be stepped on.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove any upright covers</strong> at the base. Plastic trim often hides the
            upright bolts.</li>
            <li><strong>Support each upright</strong> and remove its base bolts. Lift it straight
            up and away, watching that the cable, if it runs through this upright, slides out
            without snagging on the tube edges.</li>
            <li><strong>Bag, label and tape</strong> the bolts to each upright, and mark left and
            right.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Some treadmills have a crossbar or handlebar assembly linking the uprights, and some have
            the folding latch or a lift-assist mechanism attached to the uprights. Look at how the
            parts connect before removing anything, and if a part seems to be under tension, stop
            and check the manual.
          </p>
          <p>
            Once the uprights are off, check the cable ends for damage. If any insulation is
            nicked or a connector pin is bent, note it now, because the machine will need that
            fixing before it is used again. A damaged console cable is usually an inexpensive part
            but a frustrating fault to find later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="folding-frame" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Step 3: What About the Folding Frame?</h2>
<p>
            If the treadmill folds, decide whether to move it folded or flat, and lock or secure it
            accordingly. Do not dismantle the folding mechanism.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Moving it folded.</strong> Fold the deck up until the latch engages fully, then
            test the lock by gently trying to lower the deck. If the latch is worn or will not hold,
            do not move the machine folded. Even when the latch holds, a ratchet strap around the
            folded deck and frame is cheap insurance against it opening on a staircase or in a van.
            Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-fold-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding a treadmill</a>
            covers the latch and soft-drop mechanisms.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Moving it flat.</strong> With the console and uprights off, many treadmill
            bases are easier to carry flat than folded, because the weight stays low and the shape
            is predictable. The deck and base are one long, heavy object; carry it with one person
            at each end and the motor end leading on stairs if the manual or a mover advises it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Separating the deck from the base.</strong> On some machines the deck can be
            unbolted from the base frame, which splits the weight into two lighter pieces for a
            difficult staircase. It is a bigger job: the drive belt, the incline linkage and any
            sensor cables may all cross between the two parts. If the manual does not describe it,
            treat it as a technician or specialist mover's job.
          </p>
          <p>
            Non-folding treadmills skip this step. Their bases are often heavier and longer, which
            is why removing the console and uprights matters more for them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-hood" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Step 4: Do You Need to Remove the Motor Hood?</h2>
<p>
            Usually not. Nothing under the motor hood needs to come out to move a treadmill, and it
            is where the drive motor, the motor control board and the mains wiring live. The only
            common reasons to remove it are cleaning, a repair, or stripping the machine for
            scrap.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you do remove it, the machine must already be unplugged and you should have waited
            for the board's capacitors to discharge. The hood is normally held by a few screws along
            its sides or front; remove them and lift the hood straight up, watching for any cable
            attached to it, such as a power switch or indicator light.
          </p>
          <p>
            With the hood off, look but do not touch the board. If you are only cleaning, a vacuum
            with a soft brush attachment and a careful hand is enough, and our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">vacuuming the motor compartment</a>
            covers doing it safely.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Taking the motor out</h3>
          <p>
            Removing the drive motor means releasing the drive belt, disconnecting the motor leads
            from the controller and unbolting the motor mount. For a move it is almost never
            necessary. For a repair, it is generally a job for a technician, or for an owner
            following the manufacturer's service instructions for that model. For scrap, the
            motor is often worth separating because it contains copper, but check that the
            recycler wants it separate before spending the time. In every case, disconnect the
            controller wiring only once the machine has been unplugged and left to discharge, and
            photograph the terminals first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-and-belt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Step 5: Should You Take Off the Belt or Deck?</h2>
<p>
            Only if you are repairing or replacing them, or stripping the machine completely. The
            walking belt and deck do not need to come off for a move, and taking them off creates
            work: the belt has to be retensioned and realigned afterwards, and a deck that is not
            refitted squarely makes the belt track off-centre.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you do need to go this far, the general sequence on most machines is:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Count the turns</strong> as you loosen the rear roller adjustment bolts, and
            write the number down. That gives you a starting point for retensioning.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove the side rails and any end caps</strong> so the deck and rollers are
            accessible.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove the rear roller,</strong> then the deck bolts, and lift the deck
            out. A deck is a heavy, flat panel; two people make it easier.</li>
            <li><strong>Slide the belt off</strong> once the rollers and deck are free.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            The front roller is usually linked to the motor by the drive belt, and removing it
            involves the motor compartment, which brings back all the precautions above. Many decks
            can be flipped to use their unworn underside, which is one reason people take them
            out; check your manual to see whether yours is designed for that.
          </p>
          <p>
            Note the deck's condition while it is out. A deck worn through its surface coating in
            the foot-strike zone will wear a new belt quickly, and if you have reached this stage
            for a repair, it is the right moment to decide whether the deck needs replacing too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reassembly" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. How Do You Put a Treadmill Back Together and Test It?</h2>
<p>
            Reverse the order: base and frame first, then uprights, then console, connecting the
            cable before the console bolts go in. Then test everything at low speed before anyone
            uses it properly.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Place the base</strong> where it will live, on a level floor or mat, with
            the clearance your manual specifies.</li>
            <li><strong>Fit the uprights</strong> with the bolts from their bags, feeding the cable
            up through the correct upright as you go. Start every bolt by hand and leave them snug,
            not tight.</li>
            <li><strong>Connect the console cable</strong> with someone supporting the console, then
            seat the console and fit its bolts. Check the cable is not trapped between the console
            and the upright, which is the commonest assembly fault.</li>
            <li><strong>Tighten everything</strong> once all parts are in place, working across the
            machine rather than finishing one side first.</li>
            <li><strong>Refit the covers</strong> and check nothing is left in the bags. A leftover
            bolt means something is not secured.</li>
          </ol>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The test sequence</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Power on</strong> with the safety key in place, and check the console starts
            normally.</li>
            <li><strong>Run the belt at a slow speed with nobody on it.</strong> Watch that it tracks
            centrally and listen for scraping or knocking.</li>
            <li><strong>Test the safety key</strong> by pulling it while the belt runs slowly; the
            belt should stop.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the incline,</strong> if it has one, through its range. Some machines
            need an incline calibration after being moved or reassembled; the manual describes
            how.</li>
            <li><strong>Walk on it slowly</strong> for a few minutes before using it normally, and
            check the belt tension and tracking again afterwards.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If the console stays dark after reassembly, the cable connection is the first suspect.
            Unplug, recheck the connector and the cable route, and try again before looking for
            anything more serious.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you take apart a treadmill to move it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Unplug it, lower the incline first if powered, photograph everything, then disconnect the console cable at its connector before removing any bolts. Take off the console and uprights, bag and label the bolts for each part, and fold and strap the deck or carry the base flat. The motor, control board and deck can usually stay in place.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you take apart a NordicTrack treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The general sequence is the same as for other brands: unplug, disconnect the console cable at its connector, remove the console and uprights, and fold and lock the frame or carry the base flat. Fastener positions and folding mechanisms vary by model, so download the manual for your model number and follow its assembly section in reverse.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need to remove the motor to move a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost never. The motor and its control board stay in the base for a move, which keeps the wiring intact and avoids the risk of handling the controller. Removing the motor is a repair or scrap job. If the base is too heavy for the route, removing the console and uprights or hiring movers is usually the better answer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to disassemble a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Removing the console and uprights and preparing the frame for a move commonly takes around an hour for someone working carefully for the first time, and less with experience. Taking out the belt, deck or motor adds considerably more time. Photographing and labelling as you go adds a few minutes and saves much more at reassembly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to open the motor hood on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be, if the machine is switched off and unplugged at the wall and you wait before opening it, because capacitors on the motor control board can hold a charge after power is removed. Do not touch the board, its terminals or the mains wiring. If the treadmill is under warranty, check the terms before opening covers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why will my treadmill not turn on after I took it apart?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The console wiring harness is the usual cause. It may not be fully connected, the latch may not have clicked home, or the cable may have been pinched between the console and an upright. Unplug the machine, recheck the connector and the cable route, and check the deck is fully unfolded and the safety key is seated.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Disassembling a Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Taking a treadmill apart is straightforward. Most of the damage happens in the first five minutes or the last five.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Unbolting the uprights before disconnecting the cable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">With the bolts out, the upright and console hang on the wiring harness, and connectors tear or wires pull out of their pins. Always open the console cover and separate the harness at its connector first, then support each part before its last bolt comes out.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting all the bolts in one bag</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Treadmill bolts look alike but differ in length and thread, and a long bolt in a short hole can strip the thread or crush a cable. Use one labelled bag per part, keep washers in order on their bolts, and tape each bag to the part it came from.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Opening the motor hood straight after unplugging</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Capacitors on the control board can hold a charge after the power is removed. Wait before opening the hood, and do not touch the board, its terminals or the mains wiring at any point. For a move there is usually no reason to open the hood at all.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the cable drop inside the upright</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Once disconnected, the base end of the console cable can slide down inside the upright tube and disappear. Feeding it back up through steel tubing is slow and risks damaging it. Tape the cable to the outside of the upright or tie string to it before lifting anything away.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

